{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>Iron and bronze are your bolt</b>. Asher was at the extremity of the land of Israel, as it is written concerning it (Joshua 19:26) \"and it reached Carmel on the west,\" and it is written (ibid. v. 29) \"and its goings out were at the sea,\" and it is stated concerning it (Judges 1:31) \"Asher did not drive out the inhabitants of Acco\" — which is on the Great Sea, as they said (Genesis Rabbah 23:11), \"In the first year he came up to Acco and in the second to Joppa.\" If so, his land is the bolt of the land of Israel. Therefore he said, \"Iron and bronze are your bolt\" — that he will have doors of bronze and bars of iron, and the entire land is locked with them. It is a hint that HaShem will guard the entire land from enemy and avenger. Or he may say that strangers will not come to plunder the oil, for from his land it is brought by merchant ships to all the earth." "<b>And as your days, so your דָּבְאֶךָ</b>. What appears to me regarding this word is that it is like זָבְאֶךָ, for these letters interchange constantly: he says שָׁבְתָה מַדְהֵבָה (Isaiah 14:4) in place of מַזְהֵבָה, and in all Aramaic the same: זְבִיחָה in every tongue (Exodus 20:21), and likewise זְכִירָה in every tongue (Genesis 8:8), and likewise זָהָב (Genesis 2:11), זָקָן (Leviticus 21:5), זָנָב (Judges 15:4), זְרֵה הָלְאָה (Numbers 17:2), and many others so. And in the word זִיבָה itself, זָב and זָבָה interchange to דָלֶ\"ת in all their speech. And he says יָמַי נִזְעָכוּ (Job 17:1) in place of נִדְעָכוּ, and likewise זָחַלְתִּי וָאִירָא (Job 32:6) like דָּחַלְתִּי. And Scripture says, \"And as your days, so your זָבְאֶךָ will be\" — that your land shall flow with the oil mentioned, and you will bathe your foot in it all your days, forever. \n\nIt is also possible and correct what they explained, that it is the reverse of דַּאֲבוֹן נָפֶשׁ (Deuteronomy 28:65), and the days of old age are called דְּאָבוֹן, for they are days of evil and suffering, while the time of youth is called <b>your days</b>, for they are his to rejoice in them and gladden his heart. The meaning is: as the days of your youth, so will your old age be — a hint that his tranquility and honor will endure all the days of the earth. And I saw in the Jerusalem Targum: כְּיוֹמֵי עוּלֵימַתְהוֹן כְּיוֹמֵי סֵבַתְהוֹן — this is the very thing I explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {1 "Ramban's citation of Deuteronomy 28:65 follows the received verse numbering; the verse actually contains דַּאֲבוֹן עֵינַיִם rather than דַּאֲבוֹן נֶפֶשׁ, possibly reflecting a variant reading or paraphrase on his part."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:00:41.826098368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}